- FIXED: contour-getmat-dangling-reference: every SHAPE_* method in js_imgproc_shape()
  (js_imgproc.cpp) crashes or silently returns garbage when argv[0] is a real
  cv.Contour object (e.g. one produced by findContours(image, [], hierarchy)).
  Root cause: in the function's preamble,

    if((contour = js_contour_data(argv[0]))) {
      JSContourData<float> fcontour;
      fcontour.resize(contour->size());
      std::copy(contour->begin(), contour->end(), fcontour.begin());

      cv::Mat mat = contour_getmat(fcontour);
      src = mat;
    }

  `fcontour` is a local of the `if` block, and contour_getmat() returns a Mat
  that is a zero-copy view over fcontour.data() (by design, per this repo's
  "no copies" memory model). `mat` is also local to that `if` block. `src =
  mat` stores a pointer to `mat` inside the cv::_InputOutputArray, but both
  `mat` and the `fcontour` buffer it points into go out of scope and are
  destroyed the moment the `if` block ends - before any of the SHAPE_* switch
  cases run. Every subsequent read through `src` (arcLength, isContourConvex,
  minAreaRect, fitEllipse, convexHull, fitLine, approxPolyDP,
  minEnclosingCircle, minEnclosingTriangle, ...) is a use-after-free on a
  destroyed stack-allocated cv::Mat header, producing assertion failures
  ("depth == CV_32F || CV_32S", "count >= 0", etc.), silently-wrong results
  (boundingRect returns an empty rect), or occasionally a plausible-looking
  but unverified value. contourArea() has its own separate contour-handling
  branch with the same bug (calls contour_getmat() again into another
  block-local Mat). tests/unittests/test_imgproc.js works around this by
  converting each cv.Contour to a cv.Mat CV_32SC2 before calling any SHAPE_*
  method - the existing tests/unittests/test_contour_functions.js also never
  actually exercises a real cv.Contour object for this reason, only synthetic
  Mat CV_32SC2 contours.

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      const img = cv.Mat.zeros(240, 240, cv.CV_8UC1);
      cv.rectangle(img, {x: 20, y: 20, width: 80, height: 80}, 255, -1);

      const contours = [];
      cv.findContours(img, contours, [], cv.RETR_EXTERNAL, cv.CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE);

      cv.arcLength(contours[0], true);
      // Assertion failed: count >= 0 && (depth == CV_32F || depth == CV_32S)
      //   in function 'arcLength'
    });

  Fixed by widening `fcontour` to function scope in js_imgproc_shape() (so it
  outlives `mat`/`src` for the rest of the call, not just the `if` block), and
  by fixing a second instance of the identical pattern in
  include/js_inputoutputarray.hpp's js_cv_inputarray(): its Contour branch
  built `cv::Mat m = contour_getmat(*contour); return JSInputArray(m);` where
  `m` was local to js_cv_inputarray() itself and destroyed on return, leaving
  the caller's _InputArray dangling - this caused a segfault in
  intersectConvexConvex(contour, contour, ...) since p2 = js_cv_inputarray(ctx,
  argv[1]) hit this path. Fixed to construct directly from `*contour` (the
  persistent double-precision vector owned by the JS Contour object), matching
  the already-correct pattern in js_cv_inputoutputarray()/js_cv_outputarray().

- FIXED: contour-secondary-arg-type-mismatch: js_imgproc_shape()'s preamble only
  converted argv[0] to a float (CV_32FC2) Mat when it's a real cv.Contour -
  secondary contour arguments (argv[1] in convexityDefects/intersectConvexConvex/
  matchShapes) were instead read via js_cv_inputarray(ctx, argv[1]), which for a
  cv.Contour returns a double-precision (CV_64FC2) InputArray straight from the
  Contour's own storage. The resulting type mismatch between `src` (float) and
  `p2`/`c2` (double) tripped OpenCV assertions when both arguments are real
  cv.Contour objects (as opposed to Mat/PointVector, which this file's test
  suite uses to work around the issue). Fixed by routing argv[1] through the
  same js_shape_inputoutputarray() CV_32F conversion used for argv[0], for all
  three cases (SHAPE_CONVEXITY_DEFECTS, SHAPE_INTERSECT_CONVEX_CONVEX,
  SHAPE_MATCH_SHAPES):

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      const img = cv.Mat.zeros(240, 240, cv.CV_8UC1);
      cv.rectangle(img, {x: 20, y: 20, width: 80, height: 80}, 255, -1);

      const contours = [];
      cv.findContours(img, contours, [], cv.RETR_EXTERNAL, cv.CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE);
      const c = contours[0];

      cv.matchShapes(c, c, cv.CONTOURS_MATCH_I1, 0);
      // Bad argument: Invalid image type (must be single-channel) in function 'moments'

      const hullIdx = new cv.Mat();
      cv.convexHull(c, hullIdx, false, false);
      const defects = new cv.Mat();
      cv.convexityDefects(c, hullIdx, defects);
      // Assertion failed: npoints >= 0 in function 'convexityDefects'

      const p12 = new cv.Mat();
      cv.intersectConvexConvex(c, c, p12, true);
      // Assertion failed: p2.depth() == CV_32S || p2.depth() == CV_32F
      //   in function 'intersectConvexConvex'
    });

- FIXED: pointvectorvector-get-not-zero-copy: PointVectorVector.get(i) (and the same
  for DMatchVectorVector/KeyPointVectorVector/CharVectorVector) returns a
  plain JS array of converted elements instead of a zero-copy PointVector,
  because JSConverter<std::vector<cv::Point>>'s toJS() was replaced by the
  fully generic JSConverter<std::vector<T>> in js_vector.hpp, whose toJS()
  always builds a JS_NewArray() and converts each element individually. This
  contradicts this repo's documented "no copies, zero-copy iteration" design
  for PointVectorVector (see CLAUDE.md's Contour Migration Strategy /
  binary-compatibility notes) and means e.g. cv.findContours(img, pvv, ...)
  followed by pvv.get(i) no longer hands back something that can be passed
  straight into contourArea()/arcLength()/etc. as a PointVector - callers
  need to manually rebuild a `new cv.PointVector()` and push_back() every
  point first, as tests/unittests/test_imgproc.js does.

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      const img = cv.Mat.zeros(240, 240, cv.CV_8UC1);
      cv.rectangle(img, {x: 20, y: 20, width: 80, height: 80}, 255, -1);

      const pvv = new cv.PointVectorVector();
      cv.findContours(img, pvv, new cv.Mat(), cv.RETR_EXTERNAL, cv.CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE);

      console.log(pvv.get(0).constructor.name); // "Array", not "PointVector"
    });

  Fixed generically: JSConverter<std::vector<T>>::toJS() in js_vector.hpp now
  checks JSVector<T>::get_class_id() != 0 (i.e. a JSVector<T> class was
  registered by js_vector_init(), the same signal fromJS() already used) and,
  if so, wraps `val` in a `new JSVector<T>` and returns it via that vector's
  own toJS() instead of building a plain JS array - so PointVectorVector,
  DMatchVectorVector, KeyPointVectorVector, and CharVectorVector's get() all
  now hand back genuine PointVector/DMatchVector/KeyPointVector/CharVector
  instances.

  Fixing this exposed a second, pre-existing bug (see FIXED:
  jsvectoriterator-dangling-owner below): the temporary PointVector this
  produces would immediately segfault when iterated inline, e.g.
  `for (const p of pvv.get(i)) ...` - see that entry.

- FIXED: jsvectoriterator-dangling-owner: JSVectorIterator<T> (js_vector.hpp,
  used for `for...of` over any JSVector<T> - PointVector, MatVector, etc.)
  stored only a raw `JSVector<T>* vector` pointer, not a reference to the
  JSValue that owns it. Per the for-of spec, only the iterator object itself
  is kept alive between calls to next() - the original iterable expression
  isn't. So for any *temporary* JSVector<T> (nothing else holding a JS
  reference to it - e.g. `pvv.get(i)` called directly inside a for-of, or any
  function returning `new cv.PointVector()`), the temporary's refcount drops
  to 0 as soon as Symbol.iterator() returns the iterator object, and it gets
  finalized (`delete vector`, freeing `vec`) before the loop calls next() -
  every next() call after the first then dereferences a freed JSVector<T>/
  std::vector<T>, segfaulting. Predates the PointVectorVector.get() fix above
  but was masked until then, since get() previously returned a plain JS array
  (a built-in type with normal GC semantics, not a JSVectorIterator).

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      function makePV() {
        const pv = new cv.PointVector();
        pv.push_back({x: 1, y: 2});
        pv.push_back({x: 3, y: 4});
        return pv;
      }

      const out = new cv.PointVector();
      for (const p of makePV()) out.push_back(p); // segfault after first element
    });

  Fixed by having JSVectorIterator<T> hold a strong reference
  (JS_DupValue(ctx, this_val) in symbol_iterator(), JS_FreeValueRT() in the
  iterator's own finalizer) to the source JSVector<T> object for the
  iterator's lifetime.

- FIXED: contourarea-spurious-internal-exception: cv.contourArea(mat) (js_imgproc.cpp,
  SHAPE_CONTOUR_AREA) speculatively calls
  `JSVector<cv::Point>::fromJS(ctx, argv[0])` - the 2-argument, ctx-taking
  overload - to detect whether argv[0] is a PointVector. When argv[0] is
  anything else (e.g. a plain Mat), that overload's JS_GetOpaque2() call
  throws a TypeError internally ("PointVector object expected") via ctx, but
  the calling code only checks the returned pointer and never observes or
  clears the exception, so it's left pending and gets printed by the runtime
  later even though contourArea() goes on to compute and return the correct
  result. Cosmetic (doesn't affect the return value or throw to the caller)
  but noisy - every cv.contourArea(mat) call for a non-PointVector argument
  prints a stray "TypeError: PointVector object expected" to stderr. Using
  the ctx-less single-argument JSVector<cv::Point>::fromJS(value) overload
  instead (as e.g. js_vector_outputarray() in js_vector.cpp does) would avoid
  raising the exception in the first place.

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      const contour = new cv.Mat(4, 1, cv.CV_32SC2);
      cv.contourArea(contour); // logs a spurious "TypeError: PointVector
                                // object expected" to stderr despite
                                // returning the correct area
    });

  Fixed as a side effect of removing js_imgproc.cpp's JSContourData<double>
  usage: SHAPE_CONTOUR_AREA no longer re-detects the contour type itself
  (including the speculative PointVector probe) - it now just calls
  cv::contourArea(src, oriented) using the `src` the shared js_imgproc_shape()
  preamble already built (see FIXED: contour-getmat-dangling-reference).

- PARTIALLY FIXED: boxpoints-only-accepts-mat: cv.boxPoints(rotatedRect, dst)
  requires `dst` to be a cv.Mat or (as of the PointVector special-case below)
  a cv.PointVector - a cv.Contour destination still throws (does not crash)
  an OpenCV assertion. Root cause is inherent to cv::boxPoints() itself, not
  this binding: it writes a raw 4x2 single-channel CV_32F matrix (matching
  opencv.js/cv2's ndarray-shaped return value), not 4 points - and a
  std::vector<Point>-backed OutputArray (what a Contour/PointVector's
  destination becomes) can only be created as an Nx1 or 1xN point-shaped
  array, never a genuine 4x2 matrix. This is not new: the previous
  JSContourData<double>-based implementation required argv[1] to be a
  cv.Contour and would have hit the same cv::_OutputArray::create() shape
  assertion when boxPoints tried to create its 4x2 result into that Contour's
  vector storage.

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      const rr = new cv.RotatedRect({x: 50, y: 50}, {width: 40, height: 20}, 0);

      const pts = new cv.Mat();
      cv.boxPoints(rr, pts); // OK - 4x2 CV_32F Mat

      const pv = new cv.PointVector();
      cv.boxPoints(rr, pv); // OK - 4-element PointVector (see fix below)

      const ptsContour = new cv.Contour();
      cv.boxPoints(rr, ptsContour);
      // Assertion failed: d <= 2 && (size0 == 1 || size1 == 1 || size0*size1 == 0)
      //   in function 'create'
    });

  SHAPE_BOX_POINTS (js_imgproc.cpp) now special-cases a PointVector
  destination: it detects one via JSVector<cv::Point>::fromJS(argv[1]) (the
  ctx-less overload, so a non-PointVector argv[1] doesn't leave a stray
  pending exception the way the ctx-taking overload does - see FIXED:
  contourarea-spurious-internal-exception), computes boxPoints() into a local
  cv::Mat as before, and copies the resulting 4 float points across into the
  PointVector (rounded to int, matching PointVector's cv::Point element
  type). A cv.Contour destination isn't special-cased the same way (its
  points are double-precision, not int) and still hits the assertion above.

- FIXED: pointpolygontest-crashes-process: cv.pointPolygonTest(contour, pt,
  measureDist) (js_cv_point_polygon_test, js_imgproc.cpp) had no try/catch
  around cv::pointPolygonTest(), and that call requires CV_32S/CV_32F point
  data - a real cv.Contour's own storage is CV_64F (double), so calling it
  with a genuine Contour threw an uncaught cv::Exception that aborted the
  whole process (SIGABRT), not a catchable JS exception.

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      const c = new cv.Contour([{x: 20, y: 20}, {x: 100, y: 20}, {x: 100, y: 100}, {x: 20, y: 100}]);
      cv.pointPolygonTest(c, {x: 50, y: 50}, false); // process aborts (SIGABRT)
    });

  Fixed alongside the JSContourData<double> removal: the contour argument is
  now read through js_shape_inputoutputarray() (converts CV_64F sources to
  CV_32F, backed by a function-scope cv::Mat so the conversion outlives the
  call), and the cv::pointPolygonTest() call is now wrapped in the same
  try/catch -> js_cv_throw() pattern used elsewhere in this file.

- FIXED: getaffinetransform-plain-array-regression: cv.getAffineTransform(src,
  dst) briefly stopped accepting a plain JS array of point-likes
  (`[{x,y},{x,y},{x,y}]` or `[new cv.Point(...), ...]`) after
  TRANSFORM_GET_AFFINE_TRANSFORM (js_imgproc.cpp) was switched from
  js_contour_read() (which handled any JS-iterable of point-likes) to plain
  js_cv_inputarray() - whose own array fallback treats a JS array as a flat
  list of scalars, not as a list of points.

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      const src = [{x: 0, y: 0}, {x: 1, y: 0}, {x: 0, y: 1}];
      const dst = [{x: 0, y: 0}, {x: 2, y: 0}, {x: 0, y: 3}];
      cv.getAffineTransform(src, dst); // used to throw checkVector assertion
    });

  Fixed by adding a small local helper, js_read_point_array() (js_imgproc.cpp),
  used only by TRANSFORM_GET_AFFINE_TRANSFORM: if the argument is a plain JS
  array, reads each element via js_point_read() (accepts cv.Point instances
  or {x,y} objects) into a std::vector<cv::Point2f>, which then flows through
  the same depth-conversion path as every other source type. Verified against
  all of: Mat CV_{32,64}{F,S}C2 (3x1) and CV_{32,64}{F,S}C1 (3x2),
  PointVector, Point2fVector, plain array of cv.Point, and plain array of
  {x,y} - all 12 combinations now produce identical, correct results.

- FIXED: mat-ctor-vector-getobj-as-data-pointer: `new Mat(vectorInstance)` (js_mat.cpp, js_mat_initialize, ~line 358-376, the `array.isVector()` branch) passed `array.getObj()` as the raw pixel-data pointer to `cv::Mat(size, type, data)`. `cv::_InputArray::getObj()` returns the address of the underlying `std::vector<T>` container object itself (its identity, e.g. for aliasing checks) - not `vec.data()`. The constructed Mat therefore aliased the vector's own internal bookkeeping bytes (pointer/size/capacity) as if they were pixel data instead of the actual element buffer. The debug block above it also did `JSVector<cv::Point>::fromJS(argv[0])` unconditionally, which was null (and would crash on the following `pv->getBuffer()`/`pv->vec` dereferences) for any vector whose element type wasn't exactly `cv::Point`, even though `js_vector_inputarray()` (js_vector.cpp) already resolved the real element type generically a few lines above via `array`. Also unconditionally `return FALSE`'d, so the branch was unreachable/untested from JS.

  Fixed: the branch now reads `new(m) cv::Mat(array.size(), array.type()); array.getMat().copyTo(*m); return TRUE;` - `array.getMat()` builds the correctly-typed Mat header via OpenCV's own erased-type dispatch (no hardcoded element type needed), and `copyTo` copies the real element buffer. Still worth a regression test (`new cv.PointVector()` -> push points -> `new cv.Mat(pv)`) since nothing currently exercises this path.

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      const pv = new cv.PointVector();
      pv.push_back(new cv.Point(1, 2));
      pv.push_back(new cv.Point(3, 4));
      const m = new cv.Mat(pv); // aliases std::vector internals as pixel bytes, not [1,2,3,4]
    });

- js-typedarray-from-vector-iterator-mismatch: `js_typedarray<T>::from_vector()`
  (`include/js_typed_array.hpp`) forwards to `from_sequence(ctx, in.begin(),
  in.end())`, which calls `js_typedarray_remain()` on those iterators -
  but `js_typedarray_remain()` is only SFINAE-enabled for raw-pointer
  iterators (`std::enable_if<std::is_pointer<Iterator>::value>`).
  `std::vector<T>::begin()`/`end()` return `__gnu_cxx::__normal_iterator`
  (libstdc++'s wrapper type), not a raw `T*`, so `from_vector()` fails to
  compile for any real `std::vector<T>` - `no matching function for call
  to 'js_typedarray_remain(...)'`. Found while adding a TypedArray-receiver
  branch to `JSOutputArgument<T>` (`include/js_inputoutputarray.hpp`) and
  reaching for `from_vector()` as the obvious entry point - worked around
  there by calling `from_sequence(ctx, vec.data(), vec.data() +
  vec.size())` directly (raw pointers, so `is_pointer` SFINAE succeeds)
  instead of fixing `js_typedarray_remain()`'s SFINAE to also accept
  non-pointer random-access iterators, since that's shared machinery used
  elsewhere in this header and touching it was out of scope for that task.

    #include "include/js_typed_array.hpp"
    // T = float, JSContext* ctx already available
    std::vector<float> vec = {1.5f, 2.5f, 3.5f};
    JSValue ta = js_typedarray<float>::from_vector(ctx, vec);
    // error: no matching function for call to
    // 'js_typedarray_remain(const __gnu_cxx::__normal_iterator<...>&, ...)'

- opencvjs-mat-delete-missing: opencv.js requires every `cv.Mat` (and other
  embind value objects: `MatVector`, `RotatedRect`, ...) to be freed
  explicitly with `.delete()`, since Emscripten's wasm heap has no GC.
  qjs-opencv's design is the opposite - "no copies, mutable, finalizers do
  the work" (see qjs-opencv/CLAUDE.md's Memory model section) - QuickJS's
  GC + per-class finalizers handle cleanup. **Update (2026-08-17): only
  `cv.Mat` itself is actually missing this** - every `JSVector<T>`-based
  class (`MatVector`, `PointVector`, etc., `js_vector.hpp:289`) already has
  a harmless no-op `.delete()` method, added as part of the generic vector
  template. `Mat` (`js_mat.cpp`) is the one class with no `.delete()` at
  all, and it's by far the most heavily used: found while cross-checking
  all 80 example pages in doc/opencv-js-examples.md against qjs-opencv's
  actual exports (see the "opencv.js Example Compatibility Gaps" section in
  TODO.md), `.delete()` is called 349 times total across those 80 pages -
  the single highest-frequency opencv.js API surface qjs-opencv doesn't
  have, since literally every example calls it on every temporary Mat. Not
  fixable by binding more OpenCV functionality - this is the intended
  architectural difference. A harmless no-op `.delete()` stub on `Mat`
  would let opencv.js example code run unmodified without compromising the
  GC-based design; worth adding purely for source compatibility with
  pasted-in opencv.js snippets.

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      const m = new cv.Mat();
      m.delete(); // TypeError: not a function
    });

- FIXED: opencvjs-matfromarray-missing: opencv.js's `cv.matFromArray(rows,
  cols, type, array)` helper (`modules/js/src/helpers.js`) builds a Mat
  from a flat JS array or TypedArray in one call - qjs-opencv had no
  equivalent top-level function (closest was `new Mat(rows, cols, type,
  typedArray.buffer)`). Now implemented as `cv.matFromArray` in
  `js_cv.cpp` (`js_cv_matfromarray`, registered in `js_cv_static_funcs`):
  allocates a `cv::Mat(rows, cols, type)` and copies `array`'s elements
  into it per-element, cast to the Mat's depth (`CV_8U`...`CV_64F`),
  throwing a `TypeError` if `array` has fewer elements than the Mat needs.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let m = cv.matFromArray(2, 3, cv.CV_32FC1, [1,2,3,4,5,6]);
    // m.rows === 2, m.cols === 3, [...m.data32F] === [1,2,3,4,5,6]

- FIXED: opencvjs-termcriteria-constructor-missing: opencv.js exposes
  `new cv.TermCriteria(type, maxCount, epsilon)` as a real constructor.
  qjs-opencv has no `cv.TermCriteria` export at all - `cv::TermCriteria`
  is only ever built internally from an ad-hoc 3-element array
  (`js_cv.cpp:1083`, `crit[0]/crit[1]/crit[2]`) inside individual bound
  functions, never exposed as a constructible JS class. The `TERM_CRITERIA_
  COUNT`/`MAX_ITER`/`EPS` constants ARE exported (`js_cv.cpp:2077-2079`),
  so only the constructor itself is missing. Needed by 3 of the 80 example
  pages (`js_meanshift`, `js_camshift`, `js_optical_flow_lucas_kanade`).

  Checked all 3 against the actual OpenCV source tree
  (`../../opencv/doc/js_tutorials/js_assets/*.html`) before implementing:
  all 3 use the exact call shape `new cv.TermCriteria(cv.TERM_CRITERIA_EPS
  | cv.TERM_CRITERIA_COUNT, 10, ...)` and just pass the result straight
  through into another function - no property access on it in user code.
  `CamShift`/`meanShift` turned out to already be bound (see
  `opencv-js-api-mismatch-camshift-meanshift`, corrected above), so
  `js_camshift.html`/`js_meanshift.html` are now fully supported end to
  end; `js_optical_flow_lucas_kanade.html` still isn't, since it needs
  `cv.calcOpticalFlowPyrLK` (see `opencvjs-video-tracking-module-unbound`
  below, unaffected by this fix).

  opencv.js's own `cv.TermCriteria` (`modules/js/src/helpers.js`) is a
  plain JS constructor (`function TermCriteria() { this.type = ...; }`),
  not a real embind class - the `{type, maxCount, epsilon}` object it
  produces has no relation to how qjs-opencv's own internal consumers
  (`OTHER_KMEANS` in `js_cv.cpp`, `TRACK_CAMSHIFT`/`TRACK_MEANSHIFT` in
  `js_imgproc.cpp`) already read a criteria argument: all of them
  `js_array_to()` it as an ad-hoc `[type, maxCount, epsilon]` array.
  Implemented `js_cv_termcriteria_constructor()` (`js_cv.cpp`, registered
  via `JS_CFUNC_SPECIAL_DEF(..., constructor, ...)` since a plain
  `JS_CFUNC_DEF` entry can't be called with `new` in QuickJS) to return a
  real `Array` with the same 3 values also set as named `type`/`maxCount`/
  `epsilon` properties - satisfies both conventions with zero changes to
  any existing internal call site. Tested in
  `tests/unittests/test_termcriteria.js` (7 tests, including an end-to-end
  CamShift run) and used directly by `test_segmentation_video.js`'s
  `meanShift`/`CamShift` tests.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    const c = new cv.TermCriteria(cv.TERM_CRITERIA_EPS | cv.TERM_CRITERIA_COUNT, 10, 1);
    console.log(c.type, c.maxCount, c.epsilon); // 3 10 1
    console.log(c[0], c[1], c[2]); // 3 10 1 - still works as the ad-hoc array form too

- FIXED: opencvjs-matchtemplate-missing: `cv::matchTemplate` (`opencv2/imgproc.hpp`)
  is not bound anywhere in qjs-opencv - `grep -rn matchTemplate js_*.cpp`
  finds nothing. Blocks `js_template_matching_matchTemplate.html`.

  Checked the real example
  (`../../opencv/doc/js_tutorials/js_assets/js_template_matching_
  matchTemplate.html`) before implementing: it calls `cv.matchTemplate(src,
  templ, dst, cv.TM_CCOEFF, mask)` (mask always passed, as an empty Mat
  when unused) then `cv.minMaxLoc(dst, mask)` - `cv.minMaxLoc` was already
  bound, so this example is now fully supported end to end. Implemented as
  `js_cv_match_template()` in `js_imgproc.cpp` (own standalone function,
  not folded into the `FILTER_*` magic-dispatch group - that group's
  shared preamble treats `argv[0]` as a single `InputOutputArray`, but
  `matchTemplate` takes 3 independent arrays). An empty mask `Mat` is
  passed straight through to `cv::matchTemplate` unchanged - it already
  checks `_mask.empty()` internally, same as every other optional
  `_InputArray` in OpenCV, so no special-casing was needed. Tested in
  `tests/unittests/test_template_matching.js` (10 tests: one per `TM_*`
  mode round-tripping a known embedded patch through `matchTemplate` +
  `minMaxLoc`, the exact 5-arg opencv.js call shape, result-Mat sizing,
  and argument validation).

  Found and fixed a real, severe, unrelated bug while writing that test
  suite - see `minmaxloc-argv-out-of-bounds-read` below.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    cv.matchTemplate(img, templ, result, cv.TM_CCOEFF); // now works

- FIXED: opencvjs-tm-constants-missing: No `cv.TM_*` (`cv::TemplateMatchModes`:
  TM_SQDIFF, TM_SQDIFF_NORMED, TM_CCORR, TM_CCORR_NORMED, TM_CCOEFF,
  TM_CCOEFF_NORMED) constants are exported at all. Blocks
