  itself (opencvjs-matchtemplate-missing) is bound, since there'd still be
  no way to name the matching mode from JS.

  Fixed alongside `opencvjs-matchtemplate-missing` above: all 6 added via
  `JS_CV_CONSTANT(TM_*)` in `js_imgproc_static_funcs`, next to the existing
  `MORPH_*` constant block. Verified against native `cv::TemplateMatchModes`
  ordering (`TM_SQDIFF=0` ... `TM_CCOEFF_NORMED=5`) in
  `tests/unittests/test_template_matching.js`.

- minmaxloc-argv-out-of-bounds-read: `js_cv.cpp`'s `OTHER_MIN_MAX_LOC`
  case (backing `cv.minMaxLoc()`) supports 4 optional trailing callback
  arguments (`(minVal) => ..., (maxVal) => ..., (minLoc) => ..., (maxLoc)
  => ...`) by unconditionally reading `argv[1]` through `argv[4]` and
  checking `js_is_function()` on each - with no `argc` bound check at all:

    for(size_t i = 0; i < 4; i++)
      if(js_is_function(ctx, argv[i + 1]))
        JS_Call(ctx, argv[i + 1], JS_NULL, 1, &results[i]);

  QuickJS only guarantees `argv[]` has as many live slots as the
  function's declared `.length` (2 for `minMaxLoc`, padded with
  `undefined` up to that point) - anything beyond that is simply not part
  of the call's argv array. Every ordinary call site in this codebase
  (`cv.minMaxLoc(dst)`, `cv.minMaxLoc(dst, mask)`) reads past the end of
  that array on `argc < 5`, interpreting whatever bytes happen to follow
  as a `JSValue` and potentially dereferencing them as a heap object
  pointer inside `JS_IsFunction`. This is memory corruption, not merely a
  wrong-value bug - found because it crashed `tests/unittests/
  test_template_matching.js` with a `SIGSEGV` deep inside an *unrelated*
  later `cv.*` call (`js_cv_other()`, `argc` corrupted to `80`), i.e. the
  corruption doesn't necessarily crash at its own call site, just
  eventually. Reproducible standalone, though non-deterministically -
  crashed reliably at a fixed point in one script, silently succeeded
  6/6 and even 20/20 iterations in others depending on incidental
  heap/stack layout:

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
      const dst = new cv.Mat(); // ... populated via matchTemplate or otherwise
      cv.minMaxLoc(dst); // argc=1 here - argv[2..4] read out of bounds every time
    }
    // crashes unpredictably, sometimes several iterations in, sometimes not at all

  Fixed by guarding each read with `argc > int(i) + 1` before touching
  `argv[i + 1]`. This blocked writing `test_template_matching.js` (every
  test there calls `matchTemplate` immediately followed by `minMaxLoc`),
  so it was fixed as part of that work rather than only logged - not
  otherwise in scope for the matchTemplate/TermCriteria task that surfaced
  it.

- opencvjs-getoptimaldftsize-missing: `cv::getOptimalDFTSize` (`opencv2/
  core.hpp`) is not bound - `cv.dft()` itself is bound (js_imgproc.cpp),
  but the padding-size helper that real DFT code always calls first isn't.
  Blocks `js_fourier_transform_dft.html`.

    import('opencv').then(cv => {
      cv.getOptimalDFTSize(100); // TypeError: not a function
    });

- FIXED: opencvjs-dist-constants-missing: No `cv.DIST_*` (`cv::DistanceTypes`:
  DIST_L1, DIST_L2, DIST_C, DIST_L12, DIST_FAIR, DIST_WELSCH, DIST_HUBER)
  constants are exported, even though `cv.distanceTransform()` itself is
  already bound and takes a `distanceType` parameter
  (`js_imgproc.cpp:1211`) - callers currently have no way to name it from
  JS, only pass a raw integer. Blocks 3 of the 80 example pages
  (`js_watershed_distanceTransform.html`, `js_watershed_foreground.html`,
  `js_watershed_watershed.html`) and `cv.fitLine()`'s `distType` parameter
  the same way.

  Found and fixed while investigating `tests/unittests/
  test_contour_functions.js`'s `fitLine - Mat CV_32SC2` failure: it passes
  `cv.DIST_L2` (`undefined`), which `JS_ToInt32` coerces to `0` - not a
  valid `cv::DistanceTypes` value (`DIST_USER=-1, DIST_L1=1 ...
  DIST_HUBER=7`, no `0`) - so native `fitLine2D` threw "Unknown distance
  type". Fixed by adding all 8 values (including `DIST_USER=-1`) via
  `JS_CV_CONSTANT(DIST_*)` in `js_imgproc_static_funcs`, next to the
  `TM_*` block. Verified against the real
  `../../opencv/doc/js_tutorials/js_assets/js_contour_features_fitLine.html`
  sample: `cv.fitLine(cnt, line, cv.DIST_L2, 0, 0.01, 0.01)` now runs and
  produces the correct direction vector for a 45-degree test line
  (`vx=vy≈0.7071`).

  This did NOT make `test_contour_functions.js`'s `fitLine` test pass -
  it still fails, but now on a different, second bug in the test itself
  (not fixed, not this repo's convention to silently correct): it asserts
  `line.rows === 1 && line.cols === 4`, but native `cv::fitLine`
  (`opencv/modules/geometry/src/linefit.cpp:633`) always builds
  `Mat(4, 1, CV_32F)` - 4 rows, 1 column - never 1x4. The binding's actual
  4x1 output is correct; the real opencv.js sample above doesn't care
  either way, since it only reads the flat `line.data32F[0..3]`.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    const line = new cv.Mat();
    cv.fitLine(contour, line, cv.DIST_L2, 0, 0.01, 0.01); // was: throws "Unknown distance type"
    console.log(line.rows, line.cols); // 4 1 - correct (native shape), NOT 1x4

- FIXED: opencvjs-intersect-constants-missing: No `cv.INTERSECT_*`
  (`cv::RectanglesIntersectTypes`: INTERSECT_NONE=0, INTERSECT_PARTIAL=1,
  INTERSECT_FULL=2) constants are exported, even though
  `cv.rotatedRectangleIntersection()` itself is already bound
  (`js_imgproc.cpp:2357`) and returns exactly one of those 3 values -
  callers have no way to name the result. Found via
  `test_contour_functions.js`'s `rotatedRectangleIntersection` test, which
  compares the return value against `cv.INTERSECT_PARTIAL` (`undefined`).
  The function itself was already correct - verified it returns `1` and
  populates a correct 4-point intersection polygon
  (`(70,50),(70,70),(50,70),(50,50)`, the true 20x20 overlap) for two
  offset 40x40 `RotatedRect`s - only the named constants were missing.
  Fixed by adding all 3 via `JS_CV_CONSTANT(INTERSECT_*)` in
  `js_imgproc_static_funcs`, next to the new `DIST_*` block.
  `test_contour_functions.js`'s `rotatedRectangleIntersection` test now
  passes.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    console.log(cv.INTERSECT_PARTIAL); // was undefined, now 1

- boundingrect-test-file-wrong-expectation: `tests/unittests/
  test_contour_functions.js`'s `boundingRect - Mat CV_32SC2` test asserts
  `width===100, height===50` for a contour spanning x:[10,110], y:[20,70] -
  not a binding bug, the test's own expected values are wrong. Native
  `cv::boundingRect()` (`opencv/modules/geometry/src/geometry.cpp`'s
  `pointSetBoundingRect()`) deliberately returns `Rect(xmin, ymin, xmax -
  xmin + 1, ymax - ymin + 1)` - the source comments why: "right and bottom
  sides of the bounding rectangle are not inclusive (note +1 in width and
  height calculation)". For this contour that's `width=101, height=51`,
  which is exactly what the binding (a direct passthrough to
  `cv::boundingRect`) returns - confirmed correct, not touched. opencv.js
  wraps the same native function, so it would return 101/51 too.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    const contour = new cv.Mat(4, 1, cv.CV_32SC2);
    // corners (10,20),(110,20),(110,70),(10,70)
    const rect = cv.boundingRect(contour);
    console.log(rect.width, rect.height); // 101, 51 - correct; the test expects 100, 50

