- NOT-A-BUG: opencvjs-fs-createdatafile-not-applicable:
  `cv.FS_createDataFile(...)` is Emscripten's virtual in-memory filesystem
  API (`FS.createDataFile`), used by the 9 DNN example pages purely to
  stage a downloaded model blob where the wasm module's `cv.readNet()` can
  see it, since a browser has no real filesystem. qjs-opencv runs on a
  real filesystem (`qjsm`, native `std`/file I/O) - `cv.readNet()` (or
  whatever qjs-opencv's dnn model loader is called; see the dnn survey
  entries elsewhere in this file for the state of DNN bindings) can read
  model files directly, no staging step needed. Not a binding gap - the 9
  DNN example pages need this call deleted, not replicated, when adapted.

- uncaught-cv-exception-aborts-process-in-unguarded-modules: Most js_*.cpp
  files wrap their OpenCV calls in `try { ... } catch(const cv::Exception&
  e) { JS_ThrowInternalError(ctx, ...); }` so a bad argument turns into a
  catchable JS exception. 24 files have zero such try/catch anywhere,
  including several that call OpenCV functions with real precondition
  asserts: `js_calib3d.cpp`, `js_bg_subtractor.cpp`, `js_white_balancer.cpp`,
  `js_fast_line_detector.cpp`, `js_clahe.cpp`, `js_subdiv2d.cpp`. When one
  of those asserts fires, `cv::Exception` propagates as an uncaught C++
  exception straight through the QuickJS C call frame - `std::terminate()`
  aborts the whole process (SIGABRT), not a catchable `try/catch` in JS.
  Reproduced concretely with `js_white_balancer.cpp`'s `balanceWhite`
  (`GrayworldWB` requires `CV_8UC3`/`CV_16UC3`, has no channel check of its
  own in this binding) - a 4-channel Mat crashes the whole `qjsm` process
  instead of throwing:

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let src = new cv.Mat(50, 50, cv.CV_8UC4, new Uint8Array(50*50*4).fill(128));
    let dst = new cv.Mat();
    let wb = cv.createGrayworldWB();
    try {
      wb.balanceWhite(src, dst);
    } catch(e) {
      console.log("threw:", e.message); // never reached - process aborts first
    }

  Fix is mechanical per file: wrap the call dispatch in the same
  try/catch-and-JS_ThrowInternalError pattern already used by the other 68
  occurrences across `js_cv.cpp`, `js_imgproc.cpp`, `js_photo.cpp`, etc.

- FIXED: floodfill-rect-output-not-written-back: `cv::floodFill`'s `rect`
  output parameter (the flood-filled region's bounding box) was read into
  a local `JSRectData<int> rect` and passed to the native call as
  `rectPtr`, but the populated result was never copied back onto the
  caller's `argv[3]` object afterward (`js_imgproc.cpp`, `MISC_FLOOD_FILL`).
  opencv.js's `floodFill(image, mask, seedPoint, newVal, rect, loDiff,
  upDiff, connectivity)` mutates the passed-in `rect` object in place.
  Fixed by calling `js_rect_write(ctx, argv[3], ...)` with the native call's
  populated `rectPtr` right after `cv::floodFill()` returns, whenever
  `rectPtr` is non-null. Verified with the repro below.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let img = new cv.Mat(100, 100, cv.CV_8UC1, new Uint8Array(100*100));
    let rect = new cv.Rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
    cv.floodFill(img, new cv.Point(50, 50), [255], rect, [5], [5]);
    // rect is now {x:0, y:0, width:100, height:100} - the filled region

- FIXED: estimateaffine2d-inliers-output-discarded: `estimateAffine2D` and
  `estimateAffinePartial2D` (`js_calib3d.cpp`) both hardcoded `JSOutputArray
  inliers = cv::noArray();` and never read `argv[2]` at all - the dispatch
  jumped straight from `to` (argv[1]) to `method` (argv[3]). Passing an
  output Mat/array at position 2, as opencv.js's `estimateAffine2D(from,
  to, inliers, method, ...)` expects, was silently ignored: no error, the
  inlier mask was simply never populated. Fixed by resolving `argv[2]`
  through `js_cv_outputarray()` when present, in both functions. Also
  fixed a nearby bug found while reading this code: `ESTIMATE_AFFINE_2D`/
  `ESTIMATE_AFFINE_PARTIAL_2D`'s optional-arg parsing checked `argc > 6`
  twice - once (correctly) to read `confidence` from `argv[6]`, then again
  instead of `argc > 7` to read `refineIters` from `argv[7]`, an
  off-by-one that read one past the last real argument when called with
  exactly 7 args. Verified with the repro below (last point is a
  deliberate outlier - `inliers` now correctly marks it `0`).

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let from = cv.matFromArray(4, 1, cv.CV_32FC2, [0,0, 1,0, 0,1, 1,1]);
    let to = cv.matFromArray(4, 1, cv.CV_32FC2, [0,0, 1,0, 0,1, 100,100]);
    let inliers = new cv.Mat();
    cv.estimateAffine2D(from, to, inliers);
    // [...inliers.data] is now [1,1,0,1] - was an empty, untouched Mat before

- FIXED: feature2d-compute-keypoints-not-copied-back: `Feature2D.compute()`'s
  copy-back of the (possibly descriptor-filtered) keypoints was present in
  the source but commented out (`js_feature2d.cpp`). Descriptor extractors
  that can't compute a descriptor for some keypoints drop them internally;
  the caller's JS keypoints array was left stale (unfiltered) after the
  call, unlike `detect()` in the same file, which already copied back
  correctly, and unlike opencv.js's `compute()`. Fixed by uncommenting the
  `js_array_copy()` call (it already clears the target array internally,
  matching `detect()`'s pattern exactly). Verified against
  `tests/box_in_scene.png` (500 keypoints in, 500 descriptor rows out, no
  crash/regression - didn't happen to exercise ORB actually dropping any
  in this image, but confirms the write-back path runs correctly).

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let orb = new cv.ORB();
    let img = new cv.Mat(64, 64, cv.CV_8UC1, new Uint8Array(64*64));
    let kps = [];
    orb.detect(img, kps);
    let descriptors = new cv.Mat();
    orb.compute(img, kps, descriptors);
    // kps is now re-synced to whatever ORB actually kept descriptors for

- FIXED: tinytest-run-stops-silently-after-two-tests: `tests/unittests/tinytest.js`'s
  `run()` (line 53) is supposed to `try`/`catch` around each test and keep
  going, but `test_contour_functions.js` (and presumably every other
  `tinytest`-based suite) stops after exactly 2 of its 16 tests print
  `OK`, with a `Possibly unhandled promise rejection: TypeError: not a
  function at run (tests/unittests/tinytest.js:53)` printed *before* any
  test output (stdout/stderr ordering suggests it's a `.then()`/rejection
  queued at import time, not from a specific test body). Reproduced on an
  unmodified checkout (verified via `git stash`), so this is pre-existing,
  not caused by any change in this session - found incidentally while
  regression-checking after adding `js_ximgproc.cpp`'s edge-aware filters.
  Not investigated further at the time (out of scope for that task). Root
  cause found and fixed in a later session - see `tinytest-console-error-
  not-a-function` below: `console.error()` itself throws in this `qjsm`
  environment, and `run()`'s failure-path `catch` block called it, so the
  3rd test (`test_contour_functions.js`'s first *failing* test, not a
  fixed "test #3") threw a second, unhandled exception from inside the
  `catch`, aborting the loop. Nothing to do with promise timing.

    QUICKJS_MODULE_PATH=build/x86_64-linux-gnu qjsm tests/unittests/test_contour_functions.js
    # prints "Possibly unhandled promise rejection: TypeError: not a function"
    # then only 2 of 16 "Test: ... OK" lines, then exits - no FAILED, no summary

- FIXED: mat-mul-does-matrix-multiplication-not-elementwise: opencv.js's
  `mat.mul(other, scale)` wraps `cv::Mat::mul()` (elementwise/Hadamard
  product). This project's `.mul()` (`js_mat.cpp`, `MAT_EXPR_MUL` in
  `js_mat_expr`) executed `cv::Mat::operator*`/`operator*=` (real matrix
  multiplication) whenever the argument was another Mat, and silently
  dropped the `scale` argument in that branch. Two same-size square Mats
  "worked" and produced silently wrong numeric output; non-square/
  mismatched-inner-dimension Mats threw a `cv::Exception` where opencv.js
  would have succeeded. Fixed by routing both the in-place and
  output-argument branches through `mat.mul(*other, scale)` instead of
  `operator*`/`operator*=`; the in-place case had to write through
  `*input` rather than the (deliberately const) local `mat` reference,
  since `Mat::operator=` needs a non-const `this` while the other
  compound-assign ops (`&=`, `+=`, ...) don't. Verified elementwise on
  both square and non-square Mats, with and without `scale`, both the
  output-argument and in-place forms - see js_mat.cpp for the fix.

  Note on this binding's calling convention: `.mul()` takes `scale` as its
  *second* positional argument, before the optional output Mat -
  `mat.mul(other, scale, output)` - unlike every other MAT_EXPR op in this
  file (`add`/`sub`/`and`/`or`/`xor`/`div`), which are `mat.op(other,
  output)` with no scale slot. This predates this fix and wasn't changed.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let a = cv.matFromArray(2, 2, cv.CV_32FC1, [1,2,3,4]);
    let b = cv.matFromArray(2, 2, cv.CV_32FC1, [5,6,7,8]);
    let out = new cv.Mat();
    a.mul(b, 1.0, out);
    // [...out.data32F] is now [5,12,21,32] (elementwise), not [19,22,43,50] (matrix product)

- FIXED: moments-binaryimage-controls-input-interpretation:
  `js_imgproc.cpp`'s `MISC_MOMENTS` used `!binaryImage` to decide whether
  `argv[0]` should be parsed as a plain-array polygon (point set) or
  passed through as a raster image, unconditionally treating it as a
  polygon whenever `binaryImage` was false or omitted. Real
  `cv::moments()`/opencv.js decide points-vs-raster from the actual
  `InputArray` content instead (`checkVector(2)`, see
  `modules/geometry/src/moments.cpp`) - `binaryImage` only controls how a
  *raster* image gets thresholded. Passing a genuine grayscale `Mat` with
  `binaryImage=false` (valid opencv.js usage, for intensity-weighted image
  moments) got silently parsed as an empty JS array instead, producing
  all-zero moments with no error. Fixed by only taking the
  plain-array-of-points path when `argv[0]` is actually a plain JS array
  (which the generic `InputArray` resolver can't parse as Nx2 points
  itself); anything else - Mat, TypedArray, a `Contour`-shaped
  `CV_32SC2` Mat from `findContours`, ... - now goes straight through
  `cv::moments(src, binaryImage)`, letting OpenCV's own auto-detection
  decide. Verified all four call shapes (raster+binaryImage=false,
  raster+binaryImage=true, plain point array, `findContours`-output
  contour Mat) produce correct nonzero moments.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let img = new cv.Mat(20, 20, cv.CV_8UC1, new Uint8Array(20*20));
    for(let y = 5; y < 15; y++)
      for(let x = 5; x < 15; x++)
        img.data[y*20+x] = 255;
    let m = cv.moments(img, false);
    // m.m00 is now 25500 (10*10*255, intensity-weighted) - was 0 before

- FIXED: mat-step-drops-last-dimension: `Mat.step` (`js_mat.cpp`,
  `PROP_STEP`) looped `i < m->dims - 1`, silently dropping the last
  dimension's stride. opencv.js's `.step` (`getMatStep`,
  `modules/js/src/core_bindings.cpp`) always returns one entry per
  dimension (`i < mat.dims`). Fixed to match. Verified on both a 2D Mat
  (`CV_8UC3`, `dims=2` -> now 2-entry step `[60, 3]`, was `[60]`) and a
  genuine 3D Mat (`dims=3` -> now 3-entry step `[120, 24, 4]`, was
  `[120, 24]`).

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let m = new cv.Mat([4, 5, 6], cv.CV_32FC1);
    // [...m.step] is now [120, 24, 4] (one entry per dimension) - was [120, 24]

- FIXED: no-free-function-createclahe: `js_clahe.cpp` exported `CLAHE`
  only as a `new`-constructible class. opencv.js's whitelist exposes
  *only* the free-function form `cv.createCLAHE(clipLimit,
  tileGridSize)` (`cv::createCLAHE()` wrapped as a module-level
  function) - there is no `new cv.CLAHE(...)` in opencv.js at all.
  Ported code calling `cv.createCLAHE(...)` threw `TypeError:
  cv.createCLAHE is not a function`. Fixed by adding a `createCLAHE`
  free function (`js_create_clahe`, same default-argument handling as
  the constructor) exported alongside the existing `CLAHE` class, so
  both `cv.createCLAHE(...)` and the pre-existing `new cv.CLAHE(...)`
  now work. Updated call sites in the sibling `plot-cv` scripts that
  imported `CLAHE` by name (`scan.js`, `test-opencv.js`,
  `test-video.js`) to use `createCLAHE` instead - `test-image.js`,
  `imagePipeline.js`, and `test-cv.js` reference a bare `CLAHE`/`Mat`
  global with no matching import at all (pre-existing, unrelated
