- js-cv-inputarray-scalar-dangling-pointer: `js_cv_inputarray()`
  (`include/js_inputoutputarray.hpp:78-91`) - the generic JS-value-to-
  `cv::_InputArray` resolver used by most functions accepting a
  color/bounds/Scalar argument (e.g. `inRange`'s `lowerb`/`upperb`,
  likely others taking a plain-array Scalar) - silently returns a
  `cv::_InputArray` that references a destroyed local variable whenever
  the JS argument is a plain `Array` of 2-4 numbers. The code:

      } else if(js_is_array(ctx, value)) {
        std::vector<double> arr;
        cv::Scalar scalar;              // <- stack-local
        js_array_to(ctx, value, arr);
        if(arr.size() >= 2 && arr.size() <= 4) {
          for(size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++)
            scalar[i] = arr[i];
          return JSInputArray(scalar);  // <- _InputArray(const Matx&) stores &mtx, not a copy
        } ...

  `cv::Scalar` is `Scalar_<double>`, which derives from `Matx<double,4,1>`;
  OpenCV's `_InputArray(const Matx<_Tp,m,n>&)` constructor
  (`opencv2/core/mat.inl.hpp:133-134`) stores a raw pointer to the
  argument (`init(..., &mtx, ...)`), not a copy - it's designed for a
  `Scalar`/`Matx` that outlives the call, e.g. a real argument passed
  straight through to a `cv::` API, not one returned out of a helper
  function. Here the `scalar` local's storage is gone the moment
  `js_cv_inputarray()` returns, so the caller's `_InputArray` dereferences
  a dangling stack slot - by the time `cv::inRange()` (or whatever
  consumer) actually reads through it, the bytes have usually been
  overwritten by the next call's stack frame, typically reading back as
  all-zero. No exception, no obviously-missing output: `cv.inRange(img,
  [0,0,0], [255,255,255], mask)` (a literal full-range bound, which
  should mark every pixel in range) silently produces an all-zero mask
  instead. Confirmed the array-typed path is genuinely broken and not
  just an inRange-specific issue: the exact same bounds passed as a
  `Float64Array(4)` (`cv.Scalar.all(0)`/`.all(255)`) - which takes a
  completely different, safe code path earlier in the same function (the
  `js_is_typedarray` branch's `reinterpret_cast<cv::Scalar*>` on the
  typed array's own backing storage, which stays alive) - works
  correctly. Found while writing `tests/unittests/test_core_arithmetic.js`
  (opencv.js example-coverage tests): `js_colorspaces_inRange.html`'s
  binding list includes `cv.inRange`. Not fixed here - this is a
  foundational helper shared by many bindings, not scoped to one
  function; the fix is presumably `return JSInputArray(cv::Mat(scalar))`
  or similarly copying into caller-owned storage before returning, but
  that needs auditing every call site's assumption about the returned
  `_InputArray`'s lifetime, so flagging rather than changing inline.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let img = new cv.Mat(3, 3, cv.CV_8UC3);
    img.setTo([200, 50, 50]);
    let mask = new cv.Mat();
    cv.inRange(img, [0, 0, 0], [255, 255, 255], mask);
    // [...mask.data] is all 0 - every pixel is well within [0,0,0]-[255,255,255]
    // and should read all 255; cv.inRange(img, cv.Scalar.all(0), cv.Scalar.all(255), mask)
    // (Float64Array bounds instead of plain arrays) gives the correct all-255 result

- FIXED: calchist-requires-8-args-throws-null: `cv.calcHist()` (`js_imgproc.cpp`'s
  `js_cv_calc_hist`) hard-requires `argc >= 8` (`if(mask == nullptr ||
  hist == nullptr || argc < 8) return JS_EXCEPTION;`) even though its
  8th/9th parameters (`uniform`, `accumulate`) are otherwise read as
  optional (`bool uniform = true; ... if(argc > 7) uniform = ...`) - so
  the natural 7-argument opencv.js-style call `cv.calcHist(images,
  channels, mask, hist, dims, histSize, ranges)` (omitting `uniform`
  entirely, relying on its stated default) throws, and the 8th `uniform`
  argument must always be passed explicitly despite the default existing
  in the signature. Worse: it `return JS_EXCEPTION` without ever calling
  a `JS_Throw*()` first, so whatever the exception value happens to be
  is whatever's stale in the QuickJS exception slot - observed as the
  thrown value literally being `null` (`e.message` throws/reads as
  `undefined` on a `null` catch value) rather than an `Error` with a
  useful message. Found while writing
  `tests/unittests/test_core_arithmetic.js`; worked around there by
  passing `uniform` explicitly. Not fixed here - two small, easy,
  separable fixes (drop `uniform` from the hard `argc < 8` gate; throw a
  real `TypeError` instead of a bare `JS_EXCEPTION`) but out of scope for
  a test-writing pass.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let hist = new cv.Mat();
    try {
      cv.calcHist([cv.Mat.zeros(4, 4, cv.CV_8UC1)], [0], new cv.Mat(), hist, 1, [256], [[0, 256]]);
    } catch(e) {
      console.log(e); // null - not an Error, and would work fine if `uniform` (true) were passed as an 8th arg
    }

  Fixed by dropping `uniform` from the hard `argc < 8` gate (now `argc < 7`,
  matching the 7 required parameters) and throwing a real `TypeError` with a
  usage message via `JS_ThrowTypeError` instead of a bare `JS_EXCEPTION`.
  Verified the 7-arg call above now succeeds and returns a populated
  histogram Mat.

- FIXED: laplacian-pyrdown-pyrup-scharr-are-noop-stubs: `cv.Laplacian()`,
  `cv.pyrDown()`, `cv.pyrUp()`, and `cv.Scharr()` (`js_imgproc.cpp`'s
  `js_imgproc_filter`, cases `FILTER_LAPLACIAN`/`FILTER_PYR_DOWN`/
  `FILTER_PYR_UP`/`FILTER_SCHARR`) were all registered, exported, and
  callable with no argument-count/type errors, but their case bodies were
  **empty** (`case FILTER_LAPLACIAN: { break; }` etc - no `cv::Laplacian`/
  `cv::pyrDown`/`cv::pyrUp`/`cv::Scharr` call anywhere in any of the four
  bodies). Calling any of them left the destination `Mat` completely
  untouched (still empty if freshly constructed) and threw nothing -
  the single most dangerous failure mode (silent total no-op, not even
  "wrong output", just no computation at all). All four are referenced by
  name in `doc/opencv-js-examples.md`'s catalog
  (`js_gradients_Laplacian.html`, `js_pyramids_pyrDown.html`,
  `js_pyramids_pyrUp.html`, `js_gradients_Sobel.html` also uses
  `cv.Scharr`), so every one of those example pages silently produced a
  blank/unchanged image instead of the documented effect. Fixed by
  filling in all four bodies: `Laplacian(src, dst, ddepth, ksize=1,
  scale=1, delta=0, borderType)`, `Scharr(src, dst, ddepth, dx, dy,
  scale=1, delta=0, borderType)` (mirroring the sibling `FILTER_SOBEL`
  case two below it - same signature shape minus `Sobel`'s `ksize`),
  `pyrDown`/`pyrUp(src, dst, dstsize=Size(), borderType)`. Verified:
  `Laplacian`/`Scharr` produce a nonzero response at a synthetic
  rectangle's edge; `pyrDown` halves a 40x40 input to 20x20; `pyrUp`
  doubles it to 80x80. `tests/unittests/test_filtering_morphology.js` now
  covers all four (20/20 tests pass).

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let src = cv.Mat.zeros(20, 20, cv.CV_8UC1);
    cv.rectangle(src, {x:5,y:5,width:10,height:10}, 200, -1);
    let dst = new cv.Mat();
    cv.pyrDown(src, dst);
    // dst.rows === 10, dst.cols === 10 - was empty (0x0) before

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let src = cv.Mat.zeros(20, 20, cv.CV_8UC1);
    cv.rectangle(src, {x:5,y:5,width:10,height:10}, 200, -1);
    let dst = new cv.Mat();
    cv.pyrDown(src, dst);
    console.log(dst.empty); // true - pyrDown silently did nothing, no exception

- FIXED: tinytest-console-error-not-a-function: root-caused the
  previously-only-partially-diagnosed `tinytest-run-stops-silently-
  after-two-tests` entry above. `console.error(...)` throws `TypeError:
  not a function` unconditionally in this project's `qjsm` environment,
  regardless of arguments (`console.error('x')` alone throws) - `console`
  apparently only implements `.log`/some others, not `.error`.
  `tests/unittests/tinytest.js`'s `run()` catch block
  (`console.error('Test:', testName, 'FAILED', e); console.error(e.stack);`)
  called it on every test *failure*, so the moment any test in a suite
  failed, that `console.error` call itself threw inside the `catch`
  block of an `async` function - a second, unhandled exception that
  aborts the whole `for...in` loop silently (surfacing only as
  `Possibly unhandled promise rejection: TypeError: not a function at
  run (tinytest.js:53)` with zero indication that a real test failure
  was the trigger, and no report of which test failed or why). This is
  why `test_contour_functions.js` was observed stopping after exactly 2
  "OK"s - the 3rd test happened to be the first one that failed. Fixed
  by switching `tinytest.js`'s two `console.error` calls to
  `console.log`. Also fixed as a byproduct: the entire test suite is
  usable again for iterating on failures - every prior debugging session
  that hit this had to bypass `tinytest.js` and hand-roll a plain
  try/catch loop to see which test failed and why.

    // before: any failing test in a tinytest-based suite silently aborted
    // the whole run with no indication of which test failed:
    QUICKJS_MODULE_PATH=build/x86_64-linux-gnu qjsm tests/unittests/test_contour_functions.js
    // "Possibly unhandled promise rejection: TypeError: not a function" then nothing else

- polylines-color-requires-4-element-array: `cv.polylines()`
  (`js_draw.cpp`'s `js_draw_polylines`) reads its `color` argument via
  `js_array_to(ctx, argv[i++], color.arr)` into a fixed `double[4]` -
  unlike every other draw function in the same file (`rectangle`,
  `circle`, `line`, all of which use `js_color_read`/`js_array_to`
  patterns that tolerate 1-3 element arrays and a bare number), this one
  silently produces a fully-zeroed (invisible) color and draws nothing -
  no exception - for any array shorter than exactly 4 elements,
  including a bare number like `255` (valid, common usage for a
  grayscale image elsewhere in this codebase). Found while writing
  `tests/unittests/test_segmentation_video.js`; worked around there by
  always passing a full 4-element color array. Not fixed here - narrow,
  cosmetic-severity, easy fix (reuse `js_color_read` like the sibling
  draw functions) but out of scope for a test-writing pass.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let img = cv.Mat.zeros(40, 40, cv.CV_8UC1);
    let pts = cv.matFromArray(4, 1, cv.CV_32SC2, [5,5, 35,5, 35,35, 5,35]);
    cv.polylines(img, pts, true, 255, 2);
    // cv.countNonZero(img) === 0 - nothing drawn; [255,255,255,255] instead of 255 works

- FIXED: cv-psimpl-namespace-deleted-without-replacement: `cv.psimpl` (the 7
  polyline-simplification algorithms - `douglasPeucker`, `nthPoint`,
  `radialDistance`, `reumannWitkam`, `opheim`, `lang`,
  `perpendicularDistance`) is completely absent from the built module -
  `cv.psimpl === undefined`. This repo's own `CLAUDE.md` documents it as
  done ("All seven `psimpl` algorithms are freestanding functions under
  the `cv.psimpl.*` namespace") and `tests/unittests/test_psimpl_functions.js`
  is a real, correctly-written 9-test suite for it - but commit `c1d96d4`
  ("got rid of js_contour.[ch]pp completely", 2026-08-17) deleted
  `js_contour.cpp` (652 lines) outright with no successor file
  registering `cv.psimpl`, unlike the 16 shape-analysis functions from
  the same class, which *were* migrated to freestanding functions per
  `CLAUDE.md`'s own "Contour Migration Phase 1 complete" note. A
  `psimpl.hpp`/`line.hpp`/`line.cpp` implementation still exists in the
  tree (`include/psimpl.hpp`, `src/line.cpp`) and in the uncommitted
  `worktree-js-read-points` branch's own `js_contour.cpp` - the
  underlying algorithm code isn't gone, just its JS binding registration.
  Found because `test_psimpl_functions.js` fails all 9 of its tests once
  `tinytest-console-error-not-a-function` (above) stopped masking real
  test failures - previously this suite silently reported nothing past
  its first failure. `CLAUDE.md`'s "Already solved, don't rebuild" claim
  about psimpl is now stale and should be corrected.

  Fixed by adding a new `js_psimpl.cpp` registering the `cv.psimpl.*`
  namespace (Contour support dropped since the Contour class itself is
  gone). Accepts, each read zero-copy against the source's own backing
  buffer (no per-point conversion of the input polyline): Mat CV_32SC2,
  Mat CV_64FC2, cv.PointVector (binary-compatible with interleaved int32
  pairs), cv.Point2fVector (binary-compatible with interleaved float
  pairs), or a plain JS array (which does copy on read - no backing buffer
  to share). Returns the *same type* it was given: Mat CV_32SC2 in ->
  Mat CV_32SC2 out, Mat CV_64FC2 -> Mat CV_64FC2, PointVector ->
  PointVector, Point2fVector -> Point2fVector; a plain JS array (no
  matching C++ container to mirror) -> Mat CV_32SC2. Each algorithm runs
  with a tolerance of the source's own coordinate type (integer tolerance
  for int32 sources) via a `js_psimpl_run<T>()` template instantiated per
  coordinate type, instead of always downcasting to `double`. The
  simplification writes straight into the final output container -
  `js_psimpl_run_to_mat()`/`js_psimpl_run_to_vector()` allocate the
  destination (Mat or `JSVector<PointT>`) at the worst-case size up front
  (simplification only ever removes points) and get a raw pointer into
  *its own* storage as the algorithm's `OutputIterator`, then trim to the
  actual result count afterwards (`cv::Mat::resize()` /
  `std::vector::resize()` when shrinking only adjust the size, they don't
  reallocate or copy) - so there's no separate result buffer plus a
  memcpy into the return value.

  While reimplementing, hit two more bugs:

  1. (the original, pre-existing bug this namespace's absence had been
     masking) the deleted code built the psimpl input/output buffers via
     `reinterpret_cast<double*>(points.data())` on a `std::vector<cv::Point>`
     - i.e. reinterpreting each 8-byte (x:int32, y:int32) pair's raw bits
     as one IEEE-754 `double`, not converting the point's integer
     coordinates to a double value. psimpl's simplification routines then
     ran on that garbage, producing out-of-bounds iterator writes -
     reproduced as `double free or corruption (out)` crashing the process.
     Fixed (in the current zero-copy version) by having `js_psimpl_run<T>`
     run natively in the source's own coordinate type instead of ever
     reinterpreting through `double`.

  2. `js_mat_data_nothrow()` vs `js_mat_data2()`: the first cut of the
     Mat-vs-PointVector-vs-Point2fVector dispatch probed for a Mat via
     `js_mat_data2(ctx, argv[0])`, which wraps `JS_GetOpaque2()` - that
     call *throws* a `TypeError("Mat object expected")` (setting a
     pending exception on `ctx`) whenever the class doesn't match, even
     though the NULL return was meant to just mean "try the next type."
     Passing a `PointVector` left that stale pending exception sitting on
     the context while the PointVector branch below ran to completion and
     produced a correct result - but `qjsm` then reported the leftover
     exception anyway, as if the call had failed, right after printing the
     correct output. Reproduced with `cv.psimpl.nthPoint(pointVector, 2)`:
     prints the correct simplified size, then still prints `TypeError: Mat
     object expected` pointing at the call site. Fixed by using
     `js_mat_data_nothrow()` (wraps the non-throwing `JS_GetOpaque()`)
     for this exploratory type check instead.

  Verified: `cv.psimpl.douglasPeucker(contour, 5.0)` on a 20-point circle
  approximation now simplifies without crashing and without a leftover
  pending exception, for all four input types (Mat CV_32SC2, Mat CV_64FC2,
  PointVector, Point2fVector) plus a plain JS array, each returning the
  same type it was given (Mat CV_32SC2 in produces a Mat CV_32SC2 out,
  PointVector in produces a PointVector out, etc.);
  `tests/unittests/test_psimpl_functions.js` no longer crashes (3/9 pass:
  `douglasPeucker`, the PointVector case, and the plain-JS-array-input
  case). The other 6 test failures are the test file's own incorrect
  expectations, not binding bugs - see `psimpl-test-file-wrong-
  expectations` below.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    console.log(cv.psimpl); // now populated: douglasPeucker, nthPoint, etc.
    const contour = new cv.Mat(20, 1, cv.CV_32SC2);
    // ... fill with points ...
    const simplified = cv.psimpl.douglasPeucker(contour, 5.0); // no longer crashes

- FIXED: psimpl-test-file-wrong-expectations: `tests/unittests/test_psimpl_functions.js`
  encoded several incorrect assumptions about the `psimpl` library's actual
  behavior (verified directly against `include/psimpl.hpp`, independent of
  the qjs-opencv binding - see `cv-psimpl-namespace-deleted-without-
  replacement` above for the binding fix):
  - `reumannWitkam`, `radialDistance`, `opheim`, `lang`,
    `perpendicularDistance` were tested with `tol` values (3.0, 10) far
    smaller than the ~15.7-unit chord spacing between the test's 20
    circle-approximation points, so genuinely 0 points got removed at that
    tolerance - confirmed by calling `psimpl::simplify_radial_distance` and
    `simplify_reumann_witkam` directly in a standalone C++ program with the
    test's exact coordinates and tol=3.0: both returned all 20 points
    unchanged. The test's "expected fewer points" assertion was simply
    wrong for these tolerance values.
  - `nthPoint` with n=2 on 20 points was asserted to return exactly 10
    points, but `psimpl::simplify_nth_point` always force-keeps the last
    point of the polyline in addition to every nth one, so a correct result
    is 11 points (indices 0,2,4,...,18 plus 19) whenever the point count
    isn't itself an exact multiple of n plus one.

  Fixed by rewriting the whole file as a generated test matrix: every one
  of the 7 `cv.psimpl.*` algorithms x every one of the 5 accepted argument
  shapes (Mat CV_32SC2, Mat CV_64FC2, cv.PointVector, cv.Point2fVector,
  plain JS array) = 35 cases, plus 2 argument-validation cases (37 total).
  The shared test polyline (a 40-point sine wave, dx=2 between x-steps) and
  every tolerance-based algorithm's arguments were chosen and verified
  empirically against the actual built module - not guessed - specifically
  to avoid repeating the mistake above: dense enough that the
  distance-based routines genuinely merge points at the tolerances used,
  curved enough that the perpendicular/collinearity-based routines have
  real redundant points to remove. Each case checks: the result is an
  instance of the type that mirrors the input (per
  `cv-psimpl-namespace-deleted-without-replacement`'s type-mirroring
  contract), strictly fewer points than the input, the polyline's first
  and last point exactly preserved, and every returned point matching one
  of the original vertices (all 7 algorithms select a subsequence of
  existing vertices; none of them interpolate new points).
  `nthPoint`'s cases assert its exact, deterministic result (every nth
  point plus the forced last point, per the bug described above) rather
  than just "fewer points". Point2fVector cases compare with a small
  epsilon (cv::Point2f truncates to float32); every other type compares
  exactly, since none of the algorithms modify a coordinate's value.

- FIXED: convexitydefects-wrong-column-count: `cv.convexityDefects()` returns a
  `Mat` with the wrong number of columns per defect - opencv.js/native
  OpenCV's `convexityDefects` always returns 4 columns per row
  (`start_index, end_index, farthest_point_index, fixpt_depth`);
  observed 2 columns instead on a real concave hexagon contour. Found via
  `test_contour_functions.js`'s `convexityDefects - Mat CV_32SC2` test
  (previously silently un-reported - see `tinytest-console-error-not-a-
  function` above).

  Root cause: this repo links against OpenCV 5.0.0 (`opencv_geometry`
  module), whose `cv::convexityDefects` builds the output `Mat` with a
  row/col split of the underlying `Vec4i` elements that is inconsistent
  with OpenCV 4.x - confirmed with a standalone C++ program linked against
  both `/opt/opencv-4.7.0-x86_64` (gives `rows=N, cols=1, channels=4`, the
  documented native shape) and `/mnt/data/opt/opencv-5.0.0-x86_64` (gives
  `rows=1, cols=N, channels=4` for the same input - element count is right,
  but transposed into a row vector instead of a column vector). Neither
  native shape matches opencv.js's own convention of `rows=N, cols=4,
  channels=1` (columns, not channels, carry the 4 defect fields) - which is
  what the test suite (correctly) expects.

  Fixed `js_imgproc.cpp`'s `SHAPE_CONVEXITY_DEFECTS` case: call
  `cv::convexityDefects()` into a local intermediate `cv::Mat`, then
  `.reshape(1, int(defects.total()))` it (channel count -> 1, row count ->
  element count, letting `reshape` compute the resulting column count from
  total data size) before copying into the caller's output array. Using
  `total()` (element count) rather than trusting `rows`/`cols` individually
  makes this robust to either native row/col split. Verified:
  `test_contour_functions.js`'s `convexityDefects - Mat CV_32SC2` test now
  passes (`defects.cols === 4`); also updated
  `test_imgproc.js`'s `convexityDefects - rectangle` test, which had
  asserted the old `channels() === 4` shape unconditionally (also wrong for
  a genuinely-convex rectangle with 0 defects, where the resulting Mat is
  empty) - now checks `cols === 4 && channels() === 1` only when
  `total() > 0`.

    import * as cv from 'opencv';
    let contour = new cv.Mat(6, 1, cv.CV_32SC2);
    // ... (hexagon with one concave vertex - see test_contour_functions.js)
    let hull = new cv.Mat();
    cv.convexHull(contour, hull, false, false);
    let defects = new cv.Mat();
    cv.convexityDefects(contour, hull, defects);
    console.log(defects.cols); // 2, expected 4

